1
00:00:06,458 --> 00:00:09,583
<b>Until three years ago, we had a small garden in Anzac Cove.</b>

2
00:00:10,291 --> 00:00:12,666
<b>We were living a self-sufficient life, </b>

3
00:00:12,666 --> 00:00:14,500
<b>growing almost all of our food from the soil.</b>

4
00:00:17,125 --> 00:00:19,083
<b>Then our house was demolished.</b>

5
00:00:20,125 --> 00:00:23,041
<b>The house is gone. But the soil is still there.</b>

6
00:00:24,125 --> 00:00:26,833
<b>What remained was empty ground and a question. </b>

7
00:00:26,833 --> 00:00:29,916
<b>What does a person do with land whose house has been torn down? </b>

8
00:00:29,916 --> 00:00:31,666
<b>How can they repair this loss?</b>

9
00:00:33,416 --> 00:00:35,958
<b>One day I set out to understand this question.</b>

10
00:00:36,833 --> 00:00:40,083
<b>Not to take the land back, </b>
<b>but to learn how we reconnect with it. </b>

11
00:00:40,583 --> 00:00:42,666
<b>To learn that. </b>

12
00:00:42,958 --> 00:00:47,750
<b>And one of the first people I met </b>
<b>was someone who had turned loss into a beginning.</b>

13
00:00:51,583 --> 00:00:53,875
<b>Burhan left the city at the age of thirty. </b>

14
00:00:54,041 --> 00:00:57,250
<b>He rebuilt a ruined house in the Kaz Mountains. </b>

15
00:00:57,250 --> 00:00:59,958
<b>For him, everything begins with an honest diagnosis.</b>

16
00:00:59,958 --> 00:01:02,625
<b> The soil is exhausted, it can't carry any more.</b>

17
00:01:03,333 --> 00:01:05,208
<b>And after that comes a small beginning.</b>

18
00:01:22,833 --> 00:01:26,000
<b>Conventional agriculture keeps expanding.</b>

19
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,833
<b>The machines keep getting bigger.</b>

20
00:01:27,833 --> 00:01:32,583
<b>The inorganic substances, the chemicals poured onto the land keep increasing.</b>

21
00:01:32,583 --> 00:01:37,416
<b>Because (the soil) is exhausted, it can't carry any more, and only with more of these can we get a crop. </b>

22
00:01:37,416 --> 00:01:39,083
<b>And there is no sustainability in this.</b>

23
00:01:39,083 --> 00:01:43,875
<b>Not economically, above all not ecologically, and most frighteningly, not climatically. </b>

24
00:01:51,208 --> 00:01:51,666
<b>Regenerative agriculture is a good way for humanity to pay the price of the damage it has done to the earth</b>

25
00:01:51,666 --> 00:01:59,958
<b>Regenerative agriculture is a good way for humanity to pay the price of the damage it has done to the earth</b>

26
00:01:59,958 --> 00:02:06,708
<b>and if we can heal the ecosystem as much as we have destroyed it</b>

27
00:02:06,708 --> 00:02:11,000
<b>we have a chance to live more hopefully into the future. </b>

28
00:02:24,333 --> 00:02:29,416
<b>I'm Burhan Eltan, I'm a regenerative micro garden designer.</b>

29
00:02:36,833 --> 00:02:41,083
<b>I was born in Istanbul in 1973.</b>

30
00:02:41,083 --> 00:02:43,333
<b>I lived in Istanbul until I was thirty.</b>

31
00:02:43,333 --> 00:02:47,500
<b>Then I had a travelling adventure, about three years long.</b>

32
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:49,666
<b>That's where I met nature. </b>

33
00:02:49,666 --> 00:02:53,958
<b>When I returned to the city, I no longer wanted to stay there. But economically</b>

34
00:02:53,958 --> 00:02:59,250
<b>But economically, to build a new life I needed financing, a certain amount of money.</b>

35
00:02:59,250 --> 00:03:04,916
<b>After working intensely in the city for a while, I saved enough to make a start in the countryside.</b>

36
00:03:05,750 --> 00:03:09,458
<b>I settled in a village in the Kaz Mountains of the northern Aegean.</b>

37
00:03:09,458 --> 00:03:14,000
<b>I bought a ruined house, rebuilt it, and started a life.</b>

38
00:03:19,708 --> 00:03:24,708
<b>Of course, at first I didn't know much about production or my other needs. </b>

39
00:03:24,708 --> 00:03:30,333
<b>I mostly learned with the help of the villagers here, working alongside them. </b>

40
00:03:30,333 --> 00:03:34,458
<b>At the same time, from the publications and books I could reach from different sources, </b>

41
00:03:34,458 --> 00:03:41,875
<b>written by people around the world living the life I live who had started long before me, </b>

42
00:03:41,875 --> 00:03:45,083
<b>from the conversations with the guests who came and went, </b>

43
00:03:45,083 --> 00:03:50,875
<b>and by joining a few of the trainings just beginning in Türkiye, I picked up some basic knowledge. </b>

44
00:03:50,875 --> 00:03:56,416
<b>and slowly I internalized this knowledge by applying it in the field. </b>

45
00:03:56,416 --> 00:04:03,833
<b>I developed a way of practice that works in my own geography, my own climate, in the soil conditions here.</b>

46
00:04:17,708 --> 00:04:21,416
<b>The regeneration work we do here comes in three kinds.</b>

47
00:04:21,416 --> 00:04:23,833
<b>The first is conservation forestry.</b>

48
00:04:23,833 --> 00:04:28,333
<b>That is, we keep an area protected so it can return to its natural forest structure. </b>

49
00:04:28,333 --> 00:04:32,166
<b>We protect it against human and livestock intrusion.</b>

50
00:04:32,166 --> 00:04:36,541
<b>So the system keeps casting new seeds, producing new saplings </b>

51
00:04:36,541 --> 00:04:41,000
<b>and the forest fabric develops by diversifying.</b>

52
00:04:51,083 --> 00:04:55,250
<b>Without causing great destruction just to build a life on the land,</b>

53
00:04:55,250 --> 00:05:00,083
<b>and to change the mindset that you can't farm on less than five dönüms,</b>

54
00:05:00,083 --> 00:05:03,250
<b>in gardens of 30 square meters, 40 square meters, 50 square meters </b>

55
00:05:03,250 --> 00:05:07,458
<b>with high productivity, usually vertical growing, very well regenerated soils</b>

56
00:05:07,458 --> 00:05:15,041
<b>I design, build and consult on small gardens that can produce a high amount of food.</b>

57
00:05:15,041 --> 00:05:18,291
<b>And the name I've given these myself is</b>

58
00:05:18,291 --> 00:05:21,541
<b>R.M.G. — I call them 'Regenerative Micro Gardens'.</b>

59
00:05:26,250 --> 00:05:29,750
<b>Because they give a high yield in a very small area </b>

60
00:05:29,750 --> 00:05:34,000
<b>and because they're very easy to maintain, I call these gardens micro,</b>

61
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,375
<b>and because they consist entirely of regenerated soil and keep producing</b>

62
00:05:39,375 --> 00:05:45,916
<b>without needing any external input, I define them as ''regenerative micro gardens''.</b>

63
00:05:46,833 --> 00:05:51,333
<b>Whatever space you have at hand, whether it's concrete or gravel,</b>

64
00:05:51,333 --> 00:05:57,208
<b>I try to make it possible for you to turn it into a productive garden, simply and very quickly, with a setup time of 10-15 days,</b>

65
00:05:57,208 --> 00:06:00,125
<b>to have small food production areas you can run</b>

66
00:06:00,125 --> 00:06:04,416
<b>with a basic gardening training.</b>

67
00:06:04,833 --> 00:06:07,666
<b>When I design these gardens, my claim is this;</b>

68
00:06:07,666 --> 00:06:12,333
<b>let them be gardens a seventy-year-old
can look after on their own.</b>

69
00:06:23,083 --> 00:06:26,500
<b>This creates the first fundamental transformation in a person. </b>

70
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:29,416
<b>That is, the motivation to produce instead of consume.</b>

71
00:06:29,416 --> 00:06:32,500
<b>Right there we're already saving quite a lot of carbon.</b>

72
00:06:45,041 --> 00:06:52,458
<b>This soil regeneration let us make the production of any established garden more abundant, more productive</b>

73
00:06:52,458 --> 00:06:58,916
<b>What really raises our excitement is that the land we work on stores atmospheric carbon, </b>

74
00:06:58,916 --> 00:07:04,625
<b>that is carbon dioxide, into the soil as carbon by way of the plants</b>

75
00:07:04,625 --> 00:07:09,791
<b>This secondary benefit, benefiting the whole world rather than just that landowner,</b>

76
00:07:09,791 --> 00:07:17,291
<b>lets us do our work with high motivation and keeps our conscience very much at ease.</b>

77
00:07:28,333 --> 00:07:31,416
<b>And then there's the ''Soil Food Web'' we apply on large areas</b>

78
00:07:31,416 --> 00:07:36,916
<b>a soil regeneration we could render in Turkish
as ''Toprak Gıda Ağı''.</b>

79
00:07:36,916 --> 00:07:39,041
<b>It starts with compost. First we make compost.</b>

80
00:07:39,041 --> 00:07:41,833
<b>Then think of this compost as an inoculation. </b>

81
00:07:41,833 --> 00:07:46,583
<b>We inoculate the soil with the microorganisms inside it using different methods </b>

82
00:07:46,583 --> 00:07:51,416
<b>or we apply them to the plants in a liquid form we call tea, to protect them. </b>

83
00:07:51,416 --> 00:07:55,208
<b>This way we carry the life in here out into the field. </b>

84
00:07:55,208 --> 00:07:59,875
<b>Within a process of one to two years we make that soil regenerate, </b>

85
00:07:59,875 --> 00:08:05,833
<b>enter the cycle again without asking for anything external, fertilizer or nutrients, </b>

86
00:08:05,833 --> 00:08:10,833
<b>become able to produce its own crop, become fertile soil.</b>

87
00:08:10,833 --> 00:08:13,500
<b>Compost is our basic material.</b>

88
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:15,208
<b>People ask, is compost fertilizer;</b>

89
00:08:15,208 --> 00:08:17,500
<b>On its own it doesn't act as fertilizer.</b>

90
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:22,500
<b>That is, it isn't a nitrogen source, it doesn't carry very high nitrogen.</b>

91
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:26,875
<b>But the organisms inside it carry the surrounding nitrogen to the plants.</b>

92
00:08:26,875 --> 00:08:30,958
<b>And in a form they can take up, that is, they carry it in inorganic form.</b>

93
00:08:44,833 --> 00:08:47,333
<b>Our life here is a production-based life</b>

94
00:08:47,333 --> 00:08:50,500
<b>Consumption isn't an applauded story here. </b>

95
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:56,250
<b>We don't become happy by consuming; on the contrary, we have a way of life that becomes happy by consuming less. </b>

96
00:08:56,250 --> 00:09:03,375
<b>If, within our own systems, we can produce what we consume in our own gardens, pantries, kitchens and workshops</b>

97
00:09:03,375 --> 00:09:05,750
<b>that is our basic source of happiness.</b>

98
00:09:06,166 --> 00:09:11,333
<b>A consumer who demands food produced with regenerative agriculture has to emerge.</b>

99
00:09:11,333 --> 00:09:16,041
<b>Secondly, experienced advisory teams have to be formed who can take these conventional fields</b>

100
00:09:16,041 --> 00:09:20,833
<b>out of chemical farming and convert them to regenerative agriculture. </b>

101
00:09:23,708 --> 00:09:30,833
<b>Besides that, the material needed to do this work has to be produced. </b>

102
00:09:30,833 --> 00:09:34,333
<b>In our method its name is 'Biotam Compost'</b>

103
00:09:34,333 --> 00:09:39,750
<b>Compost has to be produced continuously and delivered to the farmer.</b>

104
00:09:42,500 --> 00:09:46,458
<b>To really understand what stage the regeneration is at</b>

105
00:09:46,458 --> 00:09:49,833
<b>Was it achieved or not, does it need to be done again?</b>

106
00:09:49,833 --> 00:09:54,791
<b>To be able to understand this we need good, small biological laboratories</b>

107
00:09:54,791 --> 00:09:59,875
<b>and teams that continuously do biological soil analysis and count the living organisms.</b>

108
00:10:09,333 --> 00:10:13,041
<b>Actually I was making a journey like this on my own. I was visiting farms as a volunteer.</b>

109
00:10:13,041 --> 00:10:18,291
<b>When I realized I could do this not alone but by building more connections</b>

110
00:10:18,291 --> 00:10:23,250
<b>TaTuTa, and a few different volunteer projects related to natural building (I joined) </b>

111
00:10:23,250 --> 00:10:29,250
<b>Then my path brought me here, to Youth to the Countryside. I got a bit involved in Burhan's Ormanya project. </b>

112
00:10:29,250 --> 00:10:34,958
<b>I strengthened my connections here like this. I settled here. Then I stayed, it's been three years.</b>

113
00:10:40,958 --> 00:10:44,583
<b>Five years ago we started a program called Youth to the Countryside</b>

114
00:10:44,583 --> 00:10:49,666
<b>Young people come, they get training. Then they come to visit as volunteers, they come to work. </b>

115
00:10:49,666 --> 00:10:56,416
<b>The relationships don't end; something like a kinship is actually born. They end up in a position like distant cousins.</b>

116
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:03,458
<b>We mixed brown material, green material and high-nitrogen material.</b>

117
00:11:04,833 --> 00:11:10,083
<b>Even if the young people work in the city, they keep coming by so as not to cut their ties with the countryside, </b>

118
00:11:10,083 --> 00:11:12,250
<b>they carry on with the activities here.</b>

119
00:11:12,833 --> 00:11:16,625
<b>And maybe some of them will run these systems one day</b>

120
00:11:16,625 --> 00:11:19,625
<b>or set up new systems of their own.</b>

121
00:11:34,916 --> 00:11:40,416
<b>Yes, people need food. Farming has to happen. We can't go back to a hunter-gatherer society</b>

122
00:11:40,416 --> 00:11:46,541
<b>But this work we do — let's first regenerate the soils, let's stop polluting the waters.</b>

123
00:11:46,541 --> 00:11:49,166
<b>Let's filter the polluted ones through healthy soils.</b>

124
00:11:49,166 --> 00:11:53,375
<b>Instead of using chemical poisons, to produce our food </b>

125
00:11:53,375 --> 00:11:57,291
<b>and to avoid poisoning ourselves, by using biological methods</b>

126
00:11:57,291 --> 00:12:01,500
<b>And this is a very simple thing. When you say biological method, something very complicated comes to mind. </b>

127
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:07,250
<b>Produce the derivatives of compost. The extract, the tea, and use these instead of those chemicals.</b>

128
00:12:07,250 --> 00:12:11,458
<b>The farmer will still apply it. The farmer will still go out to the field with the tractor.</b>

129
00:12:11,458 --> 00:12:17,541
